"render":["notebooks/supplementary-material.qmd","notebooks/model-development-and-specification.qmd","notebooks/prior-predictive-checks.qmd","notebooks/posterior-predictive-checks.qmd","notebooks/model-sensitivity.qmd","index.qmd"]},"manuscript":{"article":"index.qmd","notebooks":[{"notebook":"index.qmd","title":"Article Notebook","order":9999},{"notebook":"notebooks/supplementary-material.qmd","title":"Supplementary material"},{"notebook":"notebooks/model-development-and-specification.qmd","title":"Model development and specification"},{"notebook":"notebooks/prior-predictive-checks.qmd","title":"Prior predictive checks"},{"notebook":"notebooks/posterior-predictive-checks.qmd","title":"Posterior predictive checks"},{"notebook":"notebooks/model-sensitivity.qmd","title":"Model sensitivity analysis"}],"mecaFile":"index-meca.zip"},"bibliography":["references.bib"],"csl":"elsevier-numeric-custom.csl","crossref":{"fig-title":"Fig.","fig-prefix":"Fig.","eq-prefix":"Eq.","title-delim":"."},"format":{"html":{"toc":true,"number-sections":true,"number-depth":3,"theme":["cosmo"],"embed-resources":false,"code-link":true,"code-overflow":"wrap","code-fold":true,"css":["styles.css"]}},"editor":"visual","engines":[{"path":"C:\\PROGRA~1\\RStudio\\RESOUR~1\\app\\bin\\quarto\\share\\extension-subtrees\\julia-engine\\_extensions\\julia-engine\\julia-engine.js"}],"quarto-internal":{"subarticles":[{"input":"C:\\Users\\mafavero\\Documents\\git_repo\\thermostat-war\\index.qmd","token":"nb-article","render":true},{"input":"C:\\Users\\mafavero\\Documents\\git_repo\\thermostat-war\\notebooks\\supplementary-material.qmd","token":"nb-1","render":true},{"input":"C:\\Users\\mafavero\\Documents\\git_repo\\thermostat-war\\notebooks\\model-development-and-specification.qmd","token":"nb-2","render":true},{"input":"C:\\Users\\mafavero\\Documents\\git_repo\\thermostat-war\\notebooks\\prior-predictive-checks.qmd","token":"nb-3","render":true},{"input":"C:\\Users\\mafavero\\Documents\\git_repo\\thermostat-war\\notebooks\\posterior-predictive-checks.qmd","token":"nb-4","render":true},{"input":"C:\\Users\\mafavero\\Documents\\git_repo\\thermostat-war\\notebooks\\model-sensitivity.qmd","token":"nb-5","render":true}]},"notebook-preview-options":{"back":true},"theme":["cosmo"],"title-block-style":"manuscript","lightbox":"auto","number-depth":3,"title":"Ending the ‘thermostat wars’: a Bayesian causal framework for thermal comfort decision-making under occupant heterogeneity and uncertainty","author":[{"name":"Matteo Favero","orcid":"0000-0002-3853-2585","email":"matteo.favero08@gmail.com"},{"name":"Marika Vellei","orcid":"0000-0001-5676-5991"},{"name":"Andrew Sonta","orcid":"0000-0001-9021-0842"}],"date":"2026-10-31","date-modified":"last-modified","date-format":"DD MMMM, YYYY","abstract":"Human thermal responses are inherently variable and difficult to predict, yet thermal comfort assessments and building decisions often do not explicitly account for this heterogeneity and uncertainty when evaluating environmental interventions. This study investigates how Bayesian causal inference can improve thermal comfort assessment by quantifying intervention effects, their uncertainty, and heterogeneity in occupant responses. We applied the proposed framework to data from a historical laboratory thermal comfort experiment that contributed to the empirical foundation of Fanger’s predicted mean vote model. We defined causal estimands for hypothetical heating and cooling interventions and estimated their effects on thermal sensation while accounting for heterogeneity associated with biological sex, time of day, and exposure duration. The results showed that assumptions embedded in the predicted mean vote model, including fixed metabolic rates and steady-state representations of thermoregulation, contributed to discrepancies between predicted and observed thermal sensation. Thermal responses were not constant across occupants or over time: identical environmental interventions produced different effects by sex and exposure duration. To support engineering applications, we developed a utility-weighted dissatisfaction index to summarise predicted dissatisfaction while preserving uncertainty from the Bayesian model. This framework demonstrated that energy-saving temperature adjustments may have unequal consequences for different occupant groups. These findings highlight the importance of explicitly quantifying uncertainty and heterogeneity when evaluating thermal comfort interventions. Integrating probabilistic inference with decision-oriented performance metrics provides a transparent framework for building operations and design that better supports energy efficiency, occupant comfort, and thermal equity.\n","keywords":["thermal comfort","Bayesian causal inference","decision-making","thermal equity"],"citation":{"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Submitted to Building and Environment"},"google-scholar":true,"toc-location":"left","clear-hidden-classes":"none","remove-hidden":"all","unroll-markdown-cells":true},"extensions":{"book":{"multiFile":true}}}}